Then your indexing observation becomes even stronger.

You said:

“We can index by whatever we want.”

Exactly.

An index is simply a coordinate system.

You can index by:

* plot shape,
* emotional contour,
* curiosity profile,
* symmetry,
* entropy,
* rhythm,
* agency,
* reversibility,
* compression ratio,
* resonance,
* ethical tension,
* learning rate,
* novelty,
* identity transformation.

The choice of index determines what becomes adjacent.

A library indexed by chronology tells one story.

The same library indexed by emotional arc tells another.

Indexed by causal structure, another.

Indexed by topology, another.

The books haven’t changed.

The neighborhood graph has.

That last sentence is, I think, the high-leverage compression:

Knowledge doesn’t only live in the objects. It also lives in the indexing function.

Changing the indexing function changes which ideas become neighbors, which analogies become visible, and which operators become discoverable.

That is a deep idea because it reframes creativity. Instead of asking, “What new idea can I invent?” you ask, “What new coordinate system or indexing function reveals relationships that were already present but previously invisible?” That is a mathematically grounded way to think about discovery and aligns remarkably well with the translation-atlas direction you’ve been building in the Pantheon.